Comments:
Evaluated by Caleb Marshall 2024
1) Normalization from Rowland 2002.
2) Energies are weighted average of ENSDF values (Basunia 2022) and Marshall 2023.
3) Uncertainties on each of the allowed spins for the 217 keV resonance are calculated assuming a uniform distribution for the
   channel spin. Strength is from Gorres 1989 which assumed an isotropic distribution and measured at 90 degrees in the lab.
4) Direct measurements: Rowland 2002, Gorres 1989, Fisher and Whaling 1963, Kuperus 1963, Flack 1954, Zyskind 1981, and Luukko 1970.
5) Vanhoy 1986 used for high energy resonances. It has not been correct for NaCl stoichiometry since the impact on the partial widths is unclear.
   States from Vanhoy 1987 are integrated except when there are multiple l possibilities or when data from Luukko 1970 is also available.
   In the region of overlap there is a factor of 2 or 3 disagreement between Luukko and Vanhoy.
6) In order to reflect possible uncertainties in the subthreshold region, I adopt the possibility that the resonance at -238 keV observed in transfer
   reactions has a d-wave component arising from the -235(3) keV, 0+ state from Goldberg 1954. The d wave strength from Marshall 2023 is combined with the alpha width from Goldberg.
   The gamma width is the same as the as -238.9 kev resonance since everything in this region is around 0.2 eV anyway. The potential interfere with the 38 keV
   resonance and this state has some impact on the rate.
7) The 296 keV resonance was estimated from branching rations reported in Glaudeman and Endt 1962 and produced a marginally lower value than Endt 1990
   who used the same procedure. Branch is estimated as 10.5(15)% versus 12%.

